17:18:05,276 root DEBUG {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You will be given a SQL query and some strategies to rewrite the given SQL query. Your task is to summarize the provided query rewrite strategies into one paragraph.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\nSQL Query:\n```sql\nSELECT MIN(cn.name) AS producing_company,\n       MIN(miidx.info) AS rating,\n       MIN(t.title) AS movie\nFROM company_name AS cn,\n     company_type AS ct,\n     info_type AS it,\n     info_type AS it2,\n     kind_type AS kt,\n     movie_companies AS mc,\n     movie_info AS mi,\n     movie_info_idx AS miidx,\n     title AS t\nWHERE cn.country_code =\'[us]\'\n  AND ct.kind =\'production companies\'\n  AND it.info =\'rating\'\n  AND it2.info =\'release dates\'\n  AND kt.kind =\'movie\'\n  AND mi.movie_id = t.id\n  AND it2.id = mi.info_type_id\n  AND kt.id = t.kind_id\n  AND mc.movie_id = t.id\n  AND cn.id = mc.company_id\n  AND ct.id = mc.company_type_id\n  AND miidx.movie_id = t.id\n  AND it.id = miidx.info_type_id\n  AND mi.movie_id = miidx.movie_id\n  AND mi.movie_id = mc.movie_id\n  AND miidx.movie_id = mc.movie_id;\n```\n\nQuery Rewrite Strategies:\nQuery Rewrite 1:\n"""According to Case 2, the SQL query rewrite rule removes the inner `SELECT` layer and directly applies the aggregate functions and `GROUP BY` to the original data source. This is reflected in the rewritten logical plan, where the `LogicalAggregate` node is applied directly to the data sources (tables) without an intermediate `SELECT` clause.\n\nIn summary, the query rewrite rule applies to the given SQL query by removing the inner `SELECT` layer and directly applying the aggregate functions and `GROUP BY` to the original data source. This transformation is achieved through Case 2 of the query rewrite rule."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 2:\n"""Using the matched cases to explain the SQL query changes:\n\nThe original query has several filters applied in the WHERE clause. The query rewrite rule applies Case 1 by moving these filters into the ON clauses of the JOIN operations. This is done because the filter conditions do not reference columns from both tables involved in the join, so they can be logically applied to either input of the join to reduce the size of datasets before the join occurs.\n\nFor example, the original query has a filter condition `cn.country_code =\'[us]\'` that only references columns from the `company_name` table. This filter is moved into the ON clause of the JOIN operation with the `movie_companies` table, becoming `AND cn.id = mc.company_id AND cn.country_code =\'[us]\'`. Similarly, other filters are moved into the ON clauses of the JOIN operations.\n\nBy applying these changes, the rewritten query can take advantage of the reduced dataset sizes before the joins occur, which can improve query performance."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 3:\n"""The original query has repeated MIN function calls in the SELECT clause. We can move these functions outside of the loops by creating a temporary table or CTE that calculates the values once before the loop, and then join it with the main query. This will reduce the computational complexity and improve performance."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 4:\n"""The original query performs multiple joins on the same table to retrieve different attributes for certain conditions. We can combine these joins into a single join operation by using CASE statements to conditionally select different attributes from the table in one pass."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 5:\n"""The query can be rewritten by using subqueries to calculate the sums and counts. This approach would involve creating two subqueries, one for calculating the sum of scores in each category and another for counting the number of posts in each category. The main query would then join these subqueries with the user table to get the desired results."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 6:\n"""Another approach is to use window functions to calculate the sums and counts. This would involve using a window function like SUM() or COUNT() over a partition by category, and then ordering the results by the calculated sum or count."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 7:\n"""A third approach is to use a single query with multiple joins and aggregations. This would involve joining the user table with the post table, and then using aggregate functions like SUM() and COUNT() to calculate the desired values."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 8:\n"""The query can be rewritten by using the IN operator instead of LIKE. This would involve creating a subquery that returns the IDs of the rows that meet the condition, and then using the IN operator to filter the results."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 9:\n"""Another approach is to use a regular expression to search for the condition. This would involve using a function like REGEXP_LIKE() or LIKE() with a regular expression pattern to match the desired values."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 10:\n"""The query can be rewritten by using a single join instead of multiple joins. This would involve creating a temporary table or view that combines the data from the multiple tables, and then joining this table with the main table."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 11:\n"""Another approach is to use subqueries or derived tables to combine the data from the multiple tables. This would involve using a subquery or derived table to calculate the desired values, and then joining this result with the main table."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 12:\n"""The query can be rewritten by using a single INSERT statement instead of multiple INSERT statements. This would involve creating a temporary table or view that combines the data from the multiple tables, and then inserting this data into the main table."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 13:\n"""Another approach is to use a recursive common table expression (CTE) to generate the desired values. This would involve using a CTE to recursively generate the alphanumeric codes, and then selecting the desired values from the resulting table."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 14:\n"""The query can be rewritten by using a single INSERT statement instead of multiple INSERT statements. This would involve creating a temporary table or view that combines the data from the multiple tables, and then inserting this data into the main table."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 15:\n"""Another approach is to use a recursive common table expression (CTE) to generate the desired values. This would involve using a CTE to recursively generate the alphanumeric codes, and then selecting the desired values from the resulting table."""'}], 'response': "Here's a summary of the query rewrite strategies:\n\nThe given SQL query can be rewritten in various ways to improve performance or simplify the query. One strategy is to remove the inner SELECT layer and directly apply aggregate functions and GROUP BY to the original data source (Query Rewrite 1). Another approach is to move filter conditions from the WHERE clause into the ON clauses of JOIN operations (Query Rewrite 2) to reduce dataset sizes before joining. Additionally, repeated MIN function calls can be moved outside loops by creating a temporary table or CTE (Query Rewrite 3), and multiple joins on the same table can be combined using CASE statements (Query Rewrite 4). The query can also be rewritten using subqueries (Query Rewrites 5-8), window functions (Query Rewrites 6-7), or single queries with multiple joins and aggregations (Query Rewrite 7). Furthermore, the IN operator can replace LIKE (Query Rewrite 8), regular expressions can be used to search for conditions (Query Rewrite 9), and a single join can replace multiple joins (Query Rewrite 10). Finally, subqueries or derived tables can combine data from multiple tables (Query Rewrites 11-12), and recursive common table expressions (CTEs) can generate desired values (Query Rewrites 13-15).", 'time': 2.6530237197875977}
